//? Title: VGG: Small Kernels, Depth and a Sliding Classifier
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Image recognition needs large spatial context without making every convolution kernel large.
VGG stacks small filters to increase receptive fields and nonlinear depth. We check all three
A/D/E depth schedules, show exactly how dense classifier weights become sliding convolutions,
and expose compact tensor Adam fits on original CIFAR-10 inputs.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.2_classic_architectures/vgg.cpp`.

> The method

Configurations A/D/E have convolution counts (1,1,2,2,2), (2,2,3,3,3), and (2,2,4,4,4).
Five 2×2 stride-two pools follow blocks with widths 64/128/256/512/512; three dense layers
complete depths 11/16/19. There is no BN or LRN. ReLU follows every hidden layer and dropout
applies to the two hidden dense outputs. Two 3×3 convolutions have a 5×5 receptive box:

$$
\text{weights}(3\times3\to3\times3)=18C^2<25C^2=
\text{weights}(5\times5),\qquad r_{two}=1+2+2=5.
$$
\alttext(At equal input/intermediate/output widths, two three-by-three kernels use eighteen C squared weights versus twenty-five C squared for one five-by-five filter.)

At equal widths, three 3×3 kernels span a 7×7 field and use 27C² weights;
a single 7×7 filter uses 49C². Extra ReLUs also change the function class.
The five stride-two pooling stages leave a 7×7 map for a 224-pixel input.
FC6 can be reshaped into a 7×7 VALID kernel, with FC7/8 becoming 1×1
kernels. The dense and convolutional heads agree when applied to the
same feature window. The report checks that identity at canonical widths
and in a larger feature map, along with all stage counts and gradients.

All three CIFAR-10 models keep the VGG depths and use 32-pixel inputs,
quarter-width convolutions and 256-unit dense layers. Each fits the same
12,000 rows for ten epochs with independent padded crops and flips, dropout,
and Adam with learning rate .0005. Validation NLL on 5,000 original images
selects the checkpoint for evaluation on all 10,000 official test images.
VGG-11 and VGG-16 use initialization seed 17. For VGG-19, seed 17 gave
validation NLL 1.707930 and 31.36% test accuracy. The reported seed 29
reaches validation NLL 1.626591 and passes the 35% test sanity check.

The concept diagram below is drawn by `dm::graphics::Diagram`. Its editable C++
source is the complete block below; regeneration uses the same datamunge toolchain
as the numerical examples. Exports consume the saved local SVG.

```{cpp, file="figures/vgg_concept.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram;
  diagram.node("n0", {0.0, 0}, "Stack small filters\nfive pooling stages");
  diagram.node("n1", {5.2, 0}, "Dense classifier weights\nreshape as spatial kernels");
  diagram.node("n2", {10.4, 0}, "Compact A, D, E models\nexplicit tensor Adam");
  diagram.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
  diagram.save_svg("build/vgg_concept.svg");
}
